  4. [1mNotifications[0m — detect change, route to subscribers, deliver, mark read

  Building #4 first means inventing #1–3 as I go and rewriting them when the real ones land. So the fork is how we
  sequence, and it has real downstream consequences:

  - [1mVertical slice[0m — one spec covering the thinnest real tasks + identity substrate that makes notifications genuinely
    work. You get a demonstrably working notification end-to-end soonest, but the tasks app stays deliberately minimal
    and you'll likely re-open it later. The substrate decisions (storage shape, identity model) are the
    expensive-to-change part.
  - [1mDecompose into two cycles[0m — tasks app gets its own spec → plan → build, then notifications gets a second cycle
    designed against real code. Slower to the first notification, but notifications get designed against a data model
    that actually exists rather than one I guessed. Each cycle is cheap to re-scope.
  - [1mInterface-only design[0m — spec the notification subsystem against a stubbed task/user interface, defer the substrate.
    Cheapest now, but an untested design against an imagined interface is the thing most likely to be wrong, and stubs
    have a way of becoming permanent.

  My recommendation is the [1mvertical slice[0m. Notifications are the stated goal, and the risk in a two-cycle split is that
  cycle one builds a tasks app whose change-events and identity model don't fit what cycle two needs. Designing them
  together — but building only as much substrate as notifications actually require — keeps the goal in view and surfaces
  the substrate decisions while they're still cheap. It's also honest about the fact that we're building a small app,
  not just a feature.
